IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `mistake-domain`

§ `Singapore — unilateral mistake at common law and in equity`

-- SOURCE. Chwee Kin Keong and others v Digilandmall.com Pte Ltd [2005] SGCA 2,
-- [2005] 1 SLR(R) 502 (Chao Hick Tin JA, delivering the judgment of the Court
-- of Appeal), affirming in the result [2004] SGHC 71 (V K Rajah JC) but
-- differing from it on the law. Restated, and applied to algorithmic trading,
-- in Quoine Pte Ltd v B2C2 Ltd [2020] SGCA(I) 02.
--
-- WHY THIS DOCTRINE IS THE ONE WORTH ENCODING. England abolished the
-- equitable jurisdiction in COMMON mistake in Great Peace Shipping Ltd v
-- Tsavliris Salvage (International) Ltd [2003] QB 679, and the High Court
-- below read that as leaving no equitable jurisdiction in UNILATERAL mistake
-- either. The Court of Appeal disagreed, and in disagreeing it had to say
-- what the surviving jurisdiction requires. That statement -- constructive
-- knowledge PLUS an additional element of impropriety -- is Singapore's own,
-- and the modules beside this one exist to show that no other jurisdiction
-- here draws the line in the same place.
--
-- WHAT IS NOT ENCODED. Whether relief is granted on terms; the effect of
-- delay, affirmation or third-party rights on the right to rescind; rectifi-
-- cation; and the CONTENT of "fundamental", which the Court of Appeal
-- expressly declined to settle: "it is wholly unnecessary for us to deal with
-- the question as to what nature of mistake would constitute a serious
-- mistake sufficient to vitiate a contract" (at [34]). Fundamentality is a
-- fact supplied to this model, not a conclusion drawn by it.

§§ `The objective principle, and its exception`

-- "It is trite law that, as a general rule, a party to a contract is bound
-- even though he may have made a mistake in entering into the contract. The
-- law looks at the objective facts to determine whether a contract has come
-- into being. ... The raison d'etre behind this rule is the promotion of
-- commercial certainty." (at [30])
--
-- "However, there is an exception to this rule when the offeree knows that
-- the offeror does not intend the terms of the offer to be the natural
-- meaning of the words". (at [31])

@ref Chwee Kin Keong v Digilandmall.com Pte Ltd [2005] SGCA 2 at [34]; Quoine Pte Ltd v B2C2 Ltd [2020] SGCA(I) 02 at [13(a)]
GIVEN c IS A `Mistake case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the mistake is of a kind the doctrine reaches` IF
        (CONSIDER c's `what the mistaken party was mistaken about`
         WHEN `a term of the contract` THEN TRUE
         OTHERWISE FALSE)
    AND c's `the mistake was fundamental`

-- "It has to be a sufficiently important or fundamental mistake as to a term
-- for that to happen." (at [34]) Quoine at [13(a)] confirms the requirement
-- is shared by BOTH limbs, and records that the Court of Appeal left open
-- whether equity might one day reach further: "It was not necessary in this
-- case for the CA to determine whether unilateral mistake in equity could
-- extend beyond a mistake as to a term of the contract (at [80] and [92])."
-- That open question is fork F3 in the fork register; this model takes the
-- narrower reading, which is the one both cases decided on.

§§ `The common law limb — actual knowledge, and the contract is void`

-- "Accordingly, the law will declare void a contract which was purportedly
-- entered into where the non-mistaken party was actually aware of the mistake
-- made by the mistaken party." (at [37])
--
-- "In our opinion, it is only where the court finds that there is actual
-- knowledge that the case comes within the ambit of the common law doctrine
-- of unilateral mistake. There is no consensus ad idem. ... In the absence of
-- actual knowledge on the part of the non-mistaken party, a contract should
-- not be declared void under the common law as there would then be no reason
-- to displace the objective principle." (at [53])

@ref Chwee Kin Keong at [37] and [53]
GIVEN c IS A `Mistake case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the contract is void at common law` IF
        `the mistake is of a kind the doctrine reaches` c
    AND `the other party actually knew` c

§§ `The equitable limb — constructive knowledge PLUS impropriety`

-- The holding, in full (at [80]):
--
--   "Where the case falls within the common law doctrine of unilateral
--   mistake, there is, in effect, no contract. There will be no room for
--   equity to intervene. But where it does not and the court finds that there
--   is constructive knowledge on the part of the non-mistaken party, the
--   court would, in the exercise of its equitable jurisdiction, be entitled
--   to intervene and grant relief when it is unconscionable for the
--   non-mistaken party to insist that the contract be performed. Accordingly,
--   we accept the amicus curiae's submission that constructive knowledge
--   alone should not suffice to invoke equity. There must be an additional
--   element of impropriety. The conduct of deliberately not bringing the
--   suspicion of a possible mistake to the attention of the mistaken party
--   could constitute such impropriety."
--
-- Three conditions, and the first of them is a NEGATIVE one that is easy to
-- miss: equity is unavailable where the common law limb is made out, because
-- there is then nothing for equity to attach to. Thompson J's formulation,
-- approved at [54]: "If, for lack of consensus, no contract comes into
-- existence, there, of course, is nothing to which an equity can attach. It
-- is only in cases where the contract is not void at law that equity may
-- afford relief by declaring the contract voidable."

@ref Chwee Kin Keong at [73], [77], [78] and [80]
GIVEN c IS A `Mistake case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `there is an additional element of impropriety` IF
    CONSIDER c's `what the other party did`
    WHEN `nothing beyond accepting the bargain` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `carelessness alone`                   THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a conscious omission to disabuse`     THEN TRUE
    WHEN `deliberate steps to prevent discovery` THEN TRUE

-- The two FALSE arms are each a holding, not a default.
--
-- Carelessness: "Negligence per se, on the other hand, should not be
-- sufficient to invoke equity. Parties to a contract do not owe a duty of
-- care to each other." (at [77]) And, on the mistaken party's own
-- carelessness, which cuts the other way and is why no field of this model
-- records it as a bar: "carelessness on the part of the mistaken party does
-- not, ipso facto, disentitle that party to relief" (at [79]).
--
-- Conscious omission: "It seems to us that a conscious omission to disabuse a
-- mistaken party is itself sufficient to constitute unconscionable conduct
-- and thus justify the grant of equitable relief". (at [73]) This is the arm
-- on which Singapore is WIDER than Australia -- see au-unilateral-mistake.l4.
--
-- A note on why impropriety is asked about conduct and not about knowledge.
-- At [78] the court held that "'unconscionability' cannot be imputed based on
-- what a reasonable person would have known. It must be based on matters the
-- non-mistaken party knows ... One cannot act unconscionably if one does not
-- know of facts which could render an act so." A conscious omission
-- presupposes something consciously omitted, so the two conduct arms above
-- are only ever reached in this model together with the knowledge condition
-- in the rule below, which is the conjunction [78] requires.

@ref Chwee Kin Keong at [80]; Quoine at [13(b)], citing that court at [105]
GIVEN c IS A `Mistake case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the contract is voidable in equity` IF
        NOT `the contract is void at common law` c
    AND `the mistake is of a kind the doctrine reaches` c
    AND `the other party at least had reason to suspect` c
    AND `there is an additional element of impropriety` c

§§ `The verdict`

@export Decide what Singapore law does with a contract one party entered into by mistake — whether it is void at common law, voidable in equity, or stands. Applies Chwee Kin Keong v Digilandmall.com Pte Ltd [2005] SGCA 2.
GIVEN c IS A `Mistake case`   @desc The facts of the case, as found: what the mistake was about, whether it was fundamental, what the other party knew, and what he did about it.
GIVETH A `What becomes of the contract`
DECIDE `what Singapore law does` c IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the contract is void at common law` c   THEN `the contract is void`
        IF `the contract is voidable in equity` c   THEN `the contract is voidable at the mistaken party's election`
        OTHERWISE                                        `the contract stands`
